WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:04.000
From thin and wispy to big and puffy,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.500
clouds cover about 70% of the
planet at any given moment.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.008
But what if, in the next minute,

00:00:11.008 --> 00:00:13.400
poof, they all disappeared?

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.110
That instant might shock
onlooking astronauts

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.470
on the space station.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:20.630
But someone who is, say,
hiking in the desert

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:22.620
wouldn't know right away
that things on Earth

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.120
were about to get really bad.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:26.280
Within a few days, though,

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.580
the first warning sign
would show up: humidity.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.260
It'll be even worse if
you live by the coast.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.790
Normally, the sun's heat evaporates water,

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:36.180
mostly from the ocean,

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.290
and that water vapor is
condensed into clouds.

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.860
But if clouds are cut out
of the Earth's water cycle,

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.300
that water just hangs around in the air,

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.370
creating close to 100% humidity.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:48.690
If you were to get on a flight,

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.240
you might experience more
turbulence than usual.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:52.940
Without the protective cover of clouds

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.690
to bounce sunlight into space,

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.300
the sun will heat the Earth more,

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.240
creating more rising, uneven hot air.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.860
But a bad flight may be
the least of our worries,

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.930
because there'll be no more rain

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.771
or snow or even a light mist,

00:01:06.771 --> 00:01:08.354
and that means there won't be any way

00:01:08.354 --> 00:01:10.860
to replenish the water
sources we drink from,

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.480
like lakes, streams, rivers,
springs, and aquifers.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.100
So once last winter's snow melts,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.920
we're stuck with whatever
water we have on hand.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.670
And the clock is ticking.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:22.950
If the world were to keep up

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.940
its current water-consumption rate,

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.370
we would drain all of the
freshwater lakes and rivers

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.500
in about 23 years.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.570
So to conserve our freshwater supply,

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:34.130
humanity will have to be strategic.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:35.840
Today, the average American uses

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.350
about 80 to 100 gallons
of water every day.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.720
But saving ourselves is going to take more

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:44.630
than just skipping long
showers and laundry.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:46.790
The water we use in our
home and public places

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:50.150
accounts for only 21% of our water usage.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:51.838
The two biggest demands are actually

00:01:51.838 --> 00:01:54.371
thermoelectric power
to generate electricity

00:01:54.371 --> 00:01:56.471
and irrigation for farms.

00:01:56.471 --> 00:01:59.240
Not only do power plants
use tons of water,

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:02.600
ones that run on nuclear
fuel could spell disaster

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:05.030
if their water-cooling towers run dry.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:08.530
The 2011 Fukushima nuclear
disaster, for example,

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.930
was set off when an
earthquake knocked out power

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.240
to its water-cooling pumps.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.570
And farms are going to
need even more water

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.580
without the help of a good rain.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:18.947
This permanent drought would kill

00:02:18.947 --> 00:02:22.700
tons of wild vegetation and
animals as the land dries up.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:23.700
Within a few years,

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.400
eroding soil may kick up giant dust storms

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.473
like the Dust Bowl in the 1930s.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.150
Meanwhile, Earth's climate
will be going haywire

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:33.300
without clouds.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.160
It's hard to say when this will all happen

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.470
without some very
expensive climate modeling,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.210
but cloud expert Chris Fairall

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:42.320
did offer some quick
back-of-the-envelope calculations.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.487
Without clouds, average
surface temperatures

00:02:44.487 --> 00:02:47.950
would rise by as much
as 22 degrees Celsius.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:49.470
This extreme temperature spike

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:52.520
would not only destroy the
habitats of most flora and fauna,

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.530
killing off whatever survived the drought,

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:56.440
it would also melt the polar ice caps

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.310
and cause massive flooding
of coastal cities.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.720
You might end up being part
of the 40% of the world

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.350
that would be forced inland,

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:06.310
and your new home might
soon be in an endless desert

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:07.985
as seawater starts seeping into

00:03:07.985 --> 00:03:10.530
our fresh and precious groundwater.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.210
Sounds alarming, but there
are some silver linings

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:14.870
to a world without clouds.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.670
No more devastating
hurricanes and tornadoes,

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:20.410
or delayed flights
because of stormy weather,

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.510
or cloudy skies to ruin your stargazing.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:24.920
As the water supply dwindles,

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.158
we'll have to make some hard choices,

00:03:27.158 --> 00:03:29.320
but, hey, humans are creative.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.190
We could invent ways to
desalinate ocean water

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.720
or collect all that
water vapor from the air.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.653
And the sooner we do it, the better,

00:03:36.653 --> 00:03:39.960
because in reality, we're
already losing clouds.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.976
Unprecedented levels of CO2

00:03:41.976 --> 00:03:43.784
and the warming oceans and atmosphere

00:03:43.784 --> 00:03:46.170
are all causing clouds to thin out.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.370
So despite the silver linings,

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:51.470
it might be best to reduce
our greenhouse gas emissions

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:55.012
and try to avoid this
scary new world entirely.

